1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,300
Welcome to Xmip.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:07,230
Xmip stands for Cross OS Messaging & Integration Platform.

3
00:00:07,410 --> 00:00:13,835
It connects the systems an organization runs, and it keeps an account of every message it takes on.

4
00:00:14,015 --> 00:00:19,240
It is open source, and it runs on your own machines, with or without the internet.

5
00:00:19,420 --> 00:00:25,670
Let us follow one order through it, then see how it is configured, run, operated and extended.

6
00:00:27,850 --> 00:00:29,800
Xmip answers four people.

7
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:35,005
The CEO asks whether the business keeps moving when an exchange fails.

8
00:00:35,185 --> 00:00:38,610
The CIO asks who controls it and where it runs.

9
00:00:38,790 --> 00:00:45,565
The developer asks how an integration is built, and the operator asks what went wrong and what to do now.

10
00:00:45,745 --> 00:00:47,470
Each answer follows.

11
00:00:49,650 --> 00:01:00,800
Here is one order, followed to completion: an example flow like one Xmip's own tests run; the System Tests, which exercise the whole product, run a shorter one.

12
00:01:00,980 --> 00:01:14,505
A client's order arrives at a Receive Location, where Xmip takes messages in, and a Subscription, a filter on what a message holds, starts a Work Process Instance, one run of the flow, for this order alone.

13
00:01:14,685 --> 00:01:21,335
The Instance notes the order and keeps values of its own: the lines ordered, delivered and accepted.

14
00:01:21,515 --> 00:01:29,090
Every later message about the order carries its number, and a Correlation on that number brings each one to the same Instance.

15
00:01:29,270 --> 00:01:42,545
Goods may ship in parts: as the warehouse ships, the transporter delivers and the client answers, each part is counted, and a refused or returned part comes off the count, to be delivered again or credited.

16
00:01:42,725 --> 00:01:52,750
Until every line is accepted, the Instance waits again; then it confirms the order to the ERP, the business system that takes orders, and completes.

17
00:01:52,930 --> 00:01:56,430
If thirty days pass first, it fails as overdue.

18
00:01:56,610 --> 00:02:07,060
Operators see each Work Process's newest Instances, what each waits for and its values, and can dismiss a failed one or retry it once its cause is fixed.

19
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:12,840
Acceptance is where responsibility changes hands.

20
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:20,020
Before it, the stream, the raw bytes that arrive, belongs to its sender, and a refusal says where and why.

21
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:26,325
Once Xmip accepts it, it becomes a message, written to storage before anything acts on it.

22
00:02:26,505 --> 00:02:36,655
Each delivery to a destination is a journey, and every journey ends Completed, Failed, or Dismissed by an operator; a failed delivery is retried.

23
00:02:36,835 --> 00:02:43,360
A message nothing wanted waits in the Dead Message Queue until its routing is fixed and it is replayed.

24
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:45,390
Every step is audited.

25
00:02:47,570 --> 00:02:50,770
An integration in Xmip is text you can read.

26
00:02:50,950 --> 00:03:00,600
One TOML file, a plain-text configuration format, describes the cluster: its nodes, its routes, and what each piece of work requires.

27
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:04,205
Teams version it, compare it and review it like code.

28
00:03:04,385 --> 00:03:09,035
Every save is validated, then sliced into each node's own part.

29
00:03:11,215 --> 00:03:14,690
VS Code is where an integration is drawn.

30
00:03:14,870 --> 00:03:21,470
The designer draws the same cluster file the text holds, so a change in one is a change in the other.

31
00:03:21,650 --> 00:03:28,500
Completion, validation and renaming that follows every reference come from Xmip's own language server.

32
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:36,430
It speaks the standard Language Server Protocol, so other editors can use Xmip's rules through their own LSP clients.

33
00:03:38,610 --> 00:03:42,610
Where work runs is configuration, never a node's name.

34
00:03:42,790 --> 00:03:52,565
Here are three receiving nodes, a processing node, an executing node that receives, processes and sends at low latency, and three sending nodes.

35
00:03:52,745 --> 00:04:02,320
Each declares its roles, and its Node capabilities: words for what its environment gives it, such as a firewall opening or an installed module.

36
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:10,550
Work that requires nothing goes to any node with the role; work that names what it requires goes only to a node that has it.

37
00:04:10,730 --> 00:04:17,330
One node with every role works; more nodes are split out as security, firewalls and load need.

38
00:04:19,510 --> 00:04:22,535
The work belongs to the cluster, not to a node.

39
00:04:22,715 --> 00:04:31,065
Every node reaches Xmip Storage for all storage, and its Ledger holds every stream, message and journey until it is done.

40
00:04:31,245 --> 00:04:36,795
Nodes work by time-limited claims, so when one dies, another picks its work up.

41
00:04:36,975 --> 00:04:41,200
A primary Storage node has standbys that follow every write.

42
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:46,305
Stopped in order, it hands its writes over, and a standby takes over at once.

43
00:04:46,485 --> 00:04:50,060
One that vanishes is waited for, never guessed about.

44
00:04:52,240 --> 00:05:00,040
One plan runs four ways: as local processes, in containers, on virtual machines, and on physical machines.

45
00:05:00,220 --> 00:05:06,345
Virtual machines run on Hyper-V, on KVM with libvirt, or on VMware.

46
00:05:06,525 --> 00:05:12,100
The cluster file stays the same; which node runs where belongs to the environment.

47
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:20,955
The System Tests prove the flow as processes on one computer, and the Systems Tests prove it on virtual machines, Windows and Linux.

48
00:05:21,135 --> 00:05:26,935
Each way has its own safety and security trade-offs: Xmip advises, and you choose.

49
00:05:29,115 --> 00:05:31,265
Xmip is offline by design.

50
00:05:31,445 --> 00:05:40,520
Packages are built where the internet is reachable, then brought in, and installation, operation and the runtime need no internet at all.

51
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:44,950
Nothing at runtime checks a license or downloads a module.

52
00:05:45,130 --> 00:05:51,980
A node that may reach out says so with the Node capability online; a node without it is offline.

53
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:54,985
The networked option is there when you choose it.

54
00:05:57,165 --> 00:06:04,165
Operators choose their surface: the xmip command line, PowerShell, the web view, the desktop or the phone.

55
00:06:04,345 --> 00:06:16,020
Each reads one shared operator model, so they cannot disagree, and an MCP client, the Model Context Protocol that AI assistants speak, reads Xmip through the command line.

56
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:21,650
Health is said in words: Fine, Paused, Working, Stressed, Exhausted or Done.

57
00:06:21,830 --> 00:06:28,230
A group is Holding when something inside it needs attention, and it shows that item and its evidence.

58
00:06:30,410 --> 00:06:36,110
A Report presents data Xmip already keeps, such as the Audit and the Dead Messages.

59
00:06:36,290 --> 00:06:41,415
A Quest probes an installation first, and its Report presents what it found.

60
00:06:41,595 --> 00:06:54,620
The first is the Regulation Quest, its regulation a parameter, GDPR, Europe's data protection regulation, first: for each requirement, the evidence or the gap, and the action that closes it.

61
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:58,375
It never says compliant; that judgment stays yours.

62
00:06:58,555 --> 00:07:06,455
The Firewall, Security, Resilience and Dependency Reports sit in the same list, eleven in all, on every surface.

63
00:07:08,635 --> 00:07:15,685
Xmip is extended through one boundary, its C ABI, the binary interface every language can call.

64
00:07:15,865 --> 00:07:34,165
Your Work Processes, Transformations, Prepare steps and other units are libraries a node opens: write them in Rust, in C# with Xmip's Module package, in C or C++ against the C header, or in any language that exports a C function, and the node cannot tell one from another.

65
00:07:34,345 --> 00:07:37,720
The Xmip Designer generates Rust from a design.

66
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:46,650
Events, Xmip's notices of what happened, cross the same boundary, bound for C, C++, Java, Python and C#.

67
00:07:46,830 --> 00:07:55,355
Code on another machine reaches Xmip over its HTTP/2 API, inside Xmip's mutual TLS.

68
00:07:57,535 --> 00:08:01,410
Xmip's core is written in Rust, and Xmip is open source.

69
00:08:01,590 --> 00:08:10,465
Xmip itself is under the GNU Affero General Public License, version three or later, and it cannot be relicensed or withdrawn.

70
00:08:10,645 --> 00:08:20,745
Its module boundary, the headers, bindings and packages a module is built against, is under the Apache License, so the module you write stays yours.

71
00:08:20,925 --> 00:08:29,325
Every architectural decision is a record with its reasons, seventy-five so far, and tests hold the rules on every change.

72
00:08:31,505 --> 00:08:33,680
Xmip is measured, not promised.

73
00:08:33,860 --> 00:08:41,810
Its rule: anything it does around a message that takes more than a millisecond, beyond the load it carries, is a defect to find.

74
00:08:41,990 --> 00:08:54,090
Carrying a file in and out on one node, Xmip's own work measured 430 microseconds at the median of twenty runs, with the storage device's own sync time counted apart.

75
00:08:54,270 --> 00:09:04,145
And seventy transports run in a node today, of the eighty-eight declared, each proven sending a file to its far end, nine of them on Linux alone.

76
00:09:06,325 --> 00:09:16,950
That is Xmip: a message accepted once and accounted for, configuration you can read, work placed by what nodes declare, and the same answer on every surface.

77
00:09:17,130 --> 00:09:22,080
Xmip is in development; it is not GA, generally available, yet.

78
00:09:22,260 --> 00:09:27,735
To try it, the getting-started guide carries a file in and out on your own computer.

79
00:09:27,915 --> 00:09:29,815
Thank you for watching.
